Shoulder Impingement Syndrome
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Painful arc (60-120 degrees), Positive Neer/Hawkins signs, Pain with overhead activity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-056
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of shoulder pain causing sudden inability to lift the dominant arm or sleep, leading to profound functional distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing an acute, agonizing flare of localized shoulder pain following repetitive overhead activity, rendering the arm functionally useless and preventing sleep. Urgent assessment was required to definitively rule out an acute full-thickness rotator cuff tear or glenohumeral dislocation, and to provide immediate mechanical offloading and potent analgesia to restore basic functional safety prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My shoulder is locked in agonizing pain; every time I try to lift my arm to grab something, a sharp pain shoots down to my elbow and I drop it."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute-on-chronic exacerbation of right shoulder pain. Timeline: Insidious onset of anterior and lateral shoulder pain radiating to the mid-humerus over the past month. Acutely worsened 12 hours ago after a prolonged period of repetitive overhead lifting while painting. Patient reports a distinct, sharp catching pain specifically when lifting the arm midway up. Nocturnal aching shoulder pain is currently profound, substantially worsened by sleeping directly on the affected limb, preventing any rest. Complete inability to perform overhead reaching or throwing motions due to pain inhibition.
Functional Status: Highly impaired right upper limb; unable to perform overhead reaching, lifting, or basic grooming tasks.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep deprivation compounding acute pain crisis; risk of dropping objects due to sudden pain inhibition.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/86 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, visibly fatigued, cradling the right arm securely against the torso.
Physical Exam: Right shoulder reveals no gross deformity, erythema, or visible muscle atrophy. Focal point tenderness palpated directly over the greater tuberosity of the humerus and the subacromial space. Active forward elevation demonstrates a distinct painful arc of motion between 60 and 120 degrees. Positive Neer impingement sign producing sharp pain during forceful passive forward flexion of the internally rotated arm. Positive Hawkins-Kennedy test eliciting localized subacromial pain with passive internal rotation of a shoulder flexed to 90 degrees. True motor strength on isolated rotator cuff manual muscle testing is completely preserved despite pain inhibition. Observable scapular dyskinesis noted during dynamic arm elevation.
Environmental Observations: Painting supplies visible, confirming the repetitive overhead mechanical overload.
Medication Reconciliation: Took Paracetamol 1g and Ibuprofen 400mg with zero relief of the severe, sharp mechanical pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Shoulder Impingement Syndrome - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Full-Thickness Rotator Cuff Tear - Ruled out clinically by the absolute preservation of true motor strength against resistance on isolated testing, despite the pain.

Adhesive Capsulitis (Frozen Shoulder) - Ruled out by the preservation of full passive range of motion, specifically passive external rotation, which is typically lost in a frozen shoulder.

Cervical Radiculopathy (C5) - Ruled out by normal biceps reflexes, intact dermatomal sensation, negative Spurling's test, and pain strictly reproduced by localized shoulder impingement maneuvers.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, highly painful exacerbation of shoulder impingement following significant mechanical overload. The sudden loss of functional elevation due to pain inhibition rendered the dominant arm useless and prevented sleep. The critical clinical step was differentiating this severe inflammatory impingement from a structural full-thickness tendon rupture. Clinical examination clearly isolated the pathology to the subacromial space (positive Neer/Hawkins, painful arc) while confirming intact tendon continuity (preserved strength). Postponing medical evaluation would have exposed the patient to unmanaged severe pain and complete sleep deprivation. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed the localized inflammatory nature of the pain, ruled out surgical structural failure, and permitted the immediate application of mechanical offloading and targeted analgesia.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Naproxen 500mg BD with food for 5-7 days. Provided a short 3-day course of Codeine 30mg PRN strictly for nocturnal use to allow sleep.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Use a thick pillow to support the elbow and forearm when sitting or sleeping, keeping the arm slightly away from the body. Consequence: Prevents the inflamed bursa and tendons from being pinched tightly under the shoulder bone, leading to a rapid decrease in the constant, deep throbbing ache (negative reinforcement).
Action: Strictly lift all objects with the palm facing upward (supinated) and keep the elbows tucked into the ribs. Consequence: Complete mechanical bypassing of the impingement zone, allowing the patient to lift lightweight items securely without triggering the sharp, yielding pain (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral for scapular stabilization and rotator cuff strengthening once the acute pain subsides.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided a standard poly-sling for temporary daytime offloading.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2-3 weeks to monitor progress. Re-evaluate for ultrasound imaging or corticosteroid injection if refractory.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the shoulder suddenly becomes intensely hot, red, and swollen, if a high fever develops, or if there is a sudden, total inability to lift the arm against gravity at all.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, reflecting tendon and bursa pinching under the shoulder bone. Imaging is usually reserved for chronic cases failing conservative therapy. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute, sharp pain will improve over 1-2 weeks with the sling and modified lifting, but the underlying mechanics require months of dedicated rehabilitation to fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the entire shoulder becomes hot, red, and swollen, or if you completely lose the physical ability to lift your arm to the side.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The tendons and fluid sac in your shoulder are being severely pinched under the collarbone when you lift your arm. The overhead painting caused massive inflammation in this tight space. The anti-inflammatories will quiet the chemical pain signals, and the sling provides temporary rest.
Actionable Steps: Do not lift anything above shoulder height. Keep your elbows tucked into your ribs when lifting objects. Prop the arm on a pillow tonight to stop it from dropping backward and pinching the inflamed tissue.

SMS INFORMATION

